00:01:17.061 --> 00:01:19.438
The problem is you
have a program,

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.631
let's say with assertions,
that you wanna prove correct.

00:01:23.632 --> 00:01:27.513
And the method we're going to
follow is annotated with strong

00:01:27.514 --> 00:01:31.558
enough inductive pre and post
conditions and loop invariants.

00:01:31.559 --> 00:01:35.256
And then we generate
verification

00:01:35.257 --> 00:01:39.975
conditions which involve
loop free code and

00:01:39.976 --> 00:01:44.960
this will mean the underlying
logic can vary.

00:01:44.961 --> 00:01:50.286
And then we want to
validate the verification

00:01:50.287 --> 00:01:54.361
conditions using a logic solver.

00:01:54.362 --> 00:01:57.601
So what logic do we use for
these specifications?

00:01:57.602 --> 00:02:02.395
Actually there's quite
a lot of choices.

00:02:02.396 --> 00:02:05.390
The first kind of logics that
come to mind are first order

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:05.912
logics.

00:02:05.913 --> 00:02:09.959
But as we will see,
this is not quite enough.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.404
But traditionally if you really
want to automate everything,

00:02:14.405 --> 00:02:17.851
then you would like the
verification conditions to be

00:02:17.852 --> 00:02:20.165
essentially in
a decidable logic.

00:02:20.166 --> 00:02:22.733
And so
if it's quantifier free or

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:25.487
are the logics that
are desirable,

00:02:25.488 --> 00:02:29.707
then that will be fully
automatable and the idea is that

00:02:29.708 --> 00:02:34.411
this qualifier free logics
with some background theories.

00:02:34.412 --> 00:02:42.180
Especially if they're quantifier
free then they are combinable.

00:02:42.181 --> 00:02:46.251
If they meet some conditions,
they're combinable using those.

00:02:46.252 --> 00:02:49.657
And that gives you a wide
variety of theories that you

00:02:49.658 --> 00:02:53.142
could use and build decision
procedures that are kind

00:02:53.143 --> 00:02:55.171
of independent of each other.

00:02:55.172 --> 00:02:59.492
Now that attraction of decidable
logics is that verification

00:02:59.493 --> 00:03:02.243
after annotation is
fully automated.

00:03:02.244 --> 00:03:05.752
And that is actually quite
useful, because when you

00:03:05.753 --> 00:03:10.140
are actually verifying, you know
that you will either get back,

00:03:10.141 --> 00:03:11.921
well, time is one thing.

00:03:11.922 --> 00:03:14.441
But you'll get back
either an error, or

00:03:14.442 --> 00:03:16.601
it won't just keep on searching.

00:03:16.602 --> 00:03:21.398
If it keeps on searching, then I
don't know whether it's actually

00:03:21.399 --> 00:03:23.678
maybe I wrote something wrong.

00:03:23.679 --> 00:03:27.380
And there's a little bit
of uncertainty when you use

00:03:27.381 --> 00:03:31.941
the verification tool when you
don't have decidable objects.

00:03:31.942 --> 00:03:35.461
But I think where decidability
can also be a trap.

00:03:35.462 --> 00:03:40.851
We need powerful enough logics
to do program verification.

00:03:40.852 --> 00:03:45.454
And I think,
at least the natural logics

00:03:45.455 --> 00:03:50.466
that we can use to express
properties that we

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:55.622
wanna prove will turn
out to be undecidable.

00:03:55.623 --> 00:03:58.445
So for
instance we do need quantifiers.

00:03:58.446 --> 00:04:02.354
We need logics
perhaps over certain

00:04:02.355 --> 00:04:06.013
theories that are undecidable.

00:04:06.014 --> 00:04:10.255
And typically currently, I would
say, the reasons why we need

00:04:10.256 --> 00:04:14.178
more expressive power than
desirable logics are the state

00:04:14.179 --> 00:04:18.744
properties about unbounded parts
of your program configuration,

00:04:18.745 --> 00:04:21.305
like arrays and
data structures, and

00:04:21.306 --> 00:04:23.571
the dynamically changing heap.

00:04:23.572 --> 00:04:28.114
Or to express, and even if you
kind of go away from this by

00:04:28.115 --> 00:04:32.784
assuming certain libraries,
which manipulate state.

00:04:32.785 --> 00:04:36.496
And keep these structures for
you, in some abstract way,

00:04:36.497 --> 00:04:40.512
you still need to argue about
sequences, stats, functions,

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:44.321
pure recursive functions of
your data structure and so on.

00:04:44.322 --> 00:04:48.921
And these are typically
not going to

00:04:48.922 --> 00:04:52.301
be in decidable periods.

00:04:52.302 --> 00:04:54.885
So there is this obsession
with decidability, and

00:04:54.886 --> 00:04:56.156
I've been part of that.

00:04:56.157 --> 00:04:59.733
We've been trying to work with
decidable logics as much as

00:04:59.734 --> 00:05:00.471
possible.

00:05:00.472 --> 00:05:05.920
And decidability, as I said,
when achievable is very good.

00:05:05.921 --> 00:05:07.291
It gives you predictability.

00:05:07.292 --> 00:05:10.302
It also gives you engineering
of efficient tools because you

00:05:10.303 --> 00:05:11.551
can have competitions.

00:05:11.552 --> 00:05:14.199
And you don't have
things which I

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:18.740
think it's a little harder to
have competition if on some

00:05:18.741 --> 00:05:22.162
things you are going
to fail even proving.

00:05:22.163 --> 00:05:26.559
I think it kind of directs the
engineering task better when you

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.938
know it's a decidable problem.

00:05:28.939 --> 00:05:31.191
&gt;&gt; So decidability doesn't
mean efficiency, right?

00:05:31.192 --> 00:05:34.310
&gt;&gt; No it does not,
it's more like a heuristic for

00:05:34.311 --> 00:05:35.991
building better tools.

00:05:35.992 --> 00:05:37.661
It kind of guides you.

00:05:37.662 --> 00:05:42.813
And we have found that
to be the case that

00:05:42.814 --> 00:05:50.791
over time we've got better
solvers with decidable logics.

00:05:50.792 --> 00:05:54.046
We don't know how these
mechanics work, but

00:05:54.047 --> 00:05:55.509
it seems to be true.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:59.210
&gt;&gt; Can we say that the provers
operate in a more predictable

00:05:59.211 --> 00:06:01.511
way on deciding these fragments?

00:06:01.512 --> 00:06:02.701
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think predictability.

00:06:02.702 --> 00:06:05.650
&gt;&gt; Can we quantify that?

00:06:05.651 --> 00:06:06.650
&gt;&gt; I don't know.

00:06:06.651 --> 00:06:12.334
I don't know if you
can quantify that.

00:06:12.335 --> 00:06:15.235
&gt;&gt; Temperatures are predictable.

00:06:15.236 --> 00:06:18.455
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well it depends on
what you call predictable.

00:06:18.456 --> 00:06:20.294
I think you have a lower bar for

00:06:20.295 --> 00:06:23.694
predictability than
what that we've meant.

00:06:23.695 --> 00:06:25.464
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Let's just talk about

00:06:25.465 --> 00:06:26.106
variance.

00:06:26.107 --> 00:06:28.074
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:06:28.075 --> 00:06:30.475
it the variance greater
on a collection of?

00:06:30.476 --> 00:06:33.981
If you keep doing runs
with different random

00:06:33.982 --> 00:06:35.900
on undecidable [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:35.901 --> 00:06:40.205
&gt;&gt; So this agenda of building
decidable theories, the ultimate

00:06:40.206 --> 00:06:44.839
question is, can we, over time,
really obtain a powerful enough

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:49.081
decidable logic using which we
can prove all that we need to?

00:06:49.082 --> 00:06:52.481
And I think that
question is not clear.

00:06:52.482 --> 00:06:54.678
Everyone makes an epsilon step,

00:06:54.679 --> 00:06:58.921
does one more decidable logic
which is slightly more powerful.

00:06:58.922 --> 00:07:03.141
It's not clear where this
whole thing is headed.

00:07:03.142 --> 00:07:07.501
So in my view,
the answer seems to be no.

00:07:07.502 --> 00:07:10.562
I don't think we
are heading towards

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:14.811
the wonderful decidable
logics that will solve all

00:07:14.812 --> 00:07:18.185
the verification
problems [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:18.186 --> 00:07:21.256
&gt;&gt; I think your last slide it's
not the slight [INAUDIBLE] but

00:07:21.257 --> 00:07:22.986
they're often incomparable.

00:07:22.987 --> 00:07:25.293
&gt;&gt; They're incomparable as well.

00:07:25.294 --> 00:07:27.481
Well typically you
