00:08:00.569 --> 00:08:03.514
&gt;&gt; Right, but yeah, so
I'm being vague here so

00:08:03.515 --> 00:08:04.918
I hope that's okay.

00:08:04.919 --> 00:08:06.995
I'm not making any
concrete statements,

00:08:06.996 --> 00:08:09.245
I'm just trying to
motivate natural proofs,

00:08:09.246 --> 00:08:11.507
which is a different way
of looking at things.

00:08:11.508 --> 00:08:15.707
So the statements I
make are gonna be

00:08:15.708 --> 00:08:19.208
a little vague at this point.

00:08:19.209 --> 00:08:22.219
Okay, so we worked on
decidable logics for

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:25.984
heaps for a long time and
we had this logical strand and

00:08:25.985 --> 00:08:28.842
there'd been lots of logics for
heaps.

00:08:28.843 --> 00:08:33.180
And their logics are also based
on separation logics for heaps.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:37.820
Several decidable
fragments have emerged.

00:08:37.821 --> 00:08:43.050
But I don't see the spark
towards a powerful enough logic,

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:45.360
which is also decidable.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:53.400
So, two things which I
notice is that, as you

00:08:53.401 --> 00:08:58.130
get more and more expressive in
your logic, the constraints that

00:08:58.131 --> 00:09:02.150
you need to put in your syntax
become more and more awkward.

00:09:02.151 --> 00:09:05.150
You're not just given
a set of operators and

00:09:05.151 --> 00:09:07.140
say combine whatever you want.

00:09:07.141 --> 00:09:07.890
You should have some

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.770
bizarre conditions that
need to be satisfied.

00:09:11.771 --> 00:09:15.270
The second thing which I find
is more troubling is that

00:09:15.271 --> 00:09:19.700
the decision procedures get
more and more complex, right?

00:09:19.701 --> 00:09:20.910
By that I mean that

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:25.710
even if you had something which
you could solve earlier, right?

00:09:25.711 --> 00:09:28.750
It is true that the new decision
procedure which handles a larger

00:09:28.751 --> 00:09:29.510
logic, right.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:33.450
It takes much longer
to that same problem,

00:09:33.451 --> 00:09:35.480
that same instance, right?

00:09:35.481 --> 00:09:39.900
So the general way of
solving something, right?

00:09:39.901 --> 00:09:45.680
Makes the procedures tend
to work slower, okay?

00:09:45.681 --> 00:09:49.530
They have to solve a larger
class of problems more uniformly

00:09:49.531 --> 00:09:54.440
and so you get, at least in
our experience [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:54.441 --> 00:09:58.480
you start using all kinds
of background things,

00:09:58.481 --> 00:10:01.400
like monadic second order logic,
and stuff like that.

00:10:01.401 --> 00:10:04.450
Stitching these things up to
get more expressiveness, and

00:10:04.451 --> 00:10:08.860
then they work really badly on
even simple instances, right?

00:10:08.861 --> 00:10:12.180
And I think one should remember
that decidability is a heuristic

00:10:12.181 --> 00:10:13.910
here, automation is the goal,

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:17.179
decidability is not the goal,
right?

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.960
So here's the other
way of looking at it,

00:10:20.961 --> 00:10:23.230
which I've been working on for
several years.

00:10:23.231 --> 00:10:27.140
The idea in decidable logics is
that you want to curtail your

00:10:27.141 --> 00:10:30.530
expressive power of your logic
so that it becomes automatable.

00:10:30.531 --> 00:10:33.120
We automate reasonining.

00:10:33.121 --> 00:10:35.640
Now natural proofs,
the idea is different.

00:10:35.641 --> 00:10:40.290
It's that you allow a logic
to be quite powerful, right?

00:10:40.291 --> 00:10:42.129
You might restrict the style,
but

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:45.500
you don't typically restrict
the expressive power, right?

00:10:45.501 --> 00:10:49.631
But in order to
automate what you do,

00:10:49.632 --> 00:10:55.260
is you look for
a sub class of proofs, right?

00:10:55.261 --> 00:11:01.900
And these proofs are going to
be simpler to search for, right?

00:11:01.901 --> 00:11:03.790
And if you find a simple
proof for your theorem,

00:11:03.791 --> 00:11:05.790
you're gonna say,
yes, I found it.

00:11:05.791 --> 00:11:10.180
And if you don't find a proof,
then it's not gonna work,

00:11:10.181 --> 00:11:12.150
so you're gonna give up, right?

00:11:12.151 --> 00:11:17.150
So these are more like sound,
in complete ways, right,

00:11:17.151 --> 00:11:19.420
of solving problems, right?

00:11:19.421 --> 00:11:23.520
But we like to build robust
classes nevertheless and

00:11:23.521 --> 00:11:26.560
possibly even
a hierarchy of classes.

00:11:26.561 --> 00:11:29.340
I look for the simplest proofs
and if I don't find it,

00:11:29.341 --> 00:11:32.990
I enlarge my, the class
of proofs I search for.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.390
So two things here.

00:11:36.391 --> 00:11:37.890
Logic doesn't get awkward,
right,

00:11:37.891 --> 00:11:41.290
you write what you want,
given the style.

00:11:41.291 --> 00:11:44.460
And the proofs are not going to
get complex because we're kind

00:11:44.461 --> 00:11:46.750
of searching for
simple proofs only.

00:11:46.751 --> 00:11:49.380
&gt;&gt; So when you say if you
have a rolling class,

00:11:49.381 --> 00:11:53.010
does that mean the user's
involved with the proof?

00:11:53.011 --> 00:11:54.390
&gt;&gt; No.
&gt;&gt; So you're not gonna interact

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:56.610
&gt;&gt; No, so,

00:11:56.611 --> 00:12:00.210
typically you search for a
simple proof, you don't find it.

00:12:00.211 --> 00:12:02.850
Like, quantifier
instantiation is one, right?

00:12:02.851 --> 00:12:04.420
You could quantify more, right?

00:12:04.421 --> 00:12:06.250
You could instantiate more,
right?

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.503
&gt;&gt; That's the natural proof of-
&gt;&gt; So when you say a simple

00:12:10.504 --> 00:12:13.549
proof, does it mean the simpler
the proof is, the easier it is

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:16.375
for the user to sort of have
confidence in the proof, or-

00:12:16.376 --> 00:12:19.650
&gt;&gt; No, it's just that decision

00:12:19.651 --> 00:12:26.595
wise, it's simple to search for
a proof of that type, right?

00:12:26.596 --> 00:12:28.296
The complexity of searching for

00:12:28.297 --> 00:12:29.696
a proof of that kind is-
&gt;&gt; But

00:12:29.697 --> 00:12:31.601
that would depend on
the search right?

00:12:31.602 --> 00:12:32.741
&gt;&gt; True. Right.

00:12:32.742 --> 00:12:32.963
But

00:12:32.964 --> 00:12:35.260
it's gonna be
a subclass of proofs.

00:12:35.261 --> 00:12:38.272
I think that's the main thing,
right?

00:12:38.273 --> 00:12:44.330
Okay so we've been working on
natural proofs for some time and

00:12:44.331 --> 00:12:49.705
this is not practice as in
industry practice, right?

00:12:49.706 --> 00:12:52.925
It's basically trying to
make this work on a set of

00:12:52.926 --> 00:12:53.575
benchmarks.

00:12:54.825 --> 00:12:58.235
So what we have settled on
is really first order logic

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:02.600
with recursive definitions and
background theories.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:08.771
And we've found that this
is good enough to express

00:13:08.772 --> 00:13:11.034
properties about heaps.

00:13:11.035 --> 00:13:15.434
So we have a dialect of
separation logic called dryad

00:13:15.435 --> 00:13:18.870
that compiles into this logic.

00:13:18.871 --> 00:13:21.890
Right, I don't know how to do
automation on separation logic

00:13:21.891 --> 00:13:23.440
or other logics directly.

00:13:23.441 --> 00:13:24.810
I know only classical logic, so

00:13:24.811 --> 00:13:26.598
I'm going to stick
to classical logic.

00:13:26.599 --> 00:13:30.880
Right, so but we do have
more than first order logic.

00:13:30.881 --> 00:13:34.739
We do have first order logic
with recursive definitions,

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:35.295
right?

00:13:35.296 --> 00:13:38.921
And the heuristics
that we use to prove

00:13:38.922 --> 00:13:44.120
a theorem are what we found
in practice people do.

00:13:44.121 --> 00:13:49.490
If you give it manually
to a person to prove it,

00:13:49.491 --> 00:13:51.250
what tactics would they use?

00:13:51.251 --> 00:13:54.070
And typically what they use
in the world of recursive

00:13:54.071 --> 00:13:57.450
definition is, unfold these
recursive definitions.

00:13:57.451 --> 00:14:00.670
Right, typically a few times,
right?

00:14:00.671 --> 00:14:04.130
And then they do some kind
of unification, right?

00:14:04.131 --> 00:14:05.780
At the level of term rewriting,

00:14:05.781 --> 00:14:07.590
you can think of
this as unification.

00:14:07.591 --> 00:14:09.230
But it's basically,

00:14:09.231 --> 00:14:13.995
you can kind of mimic this as
interpreting the recursive

00:14:13.996 --> 00:14:17.810
definitions that you have as
uninterpreted functions, right?

00:14:17.811 --> 00:14:25.320
That kind of mimics the
unification things that they do.

00:14:25.321 --> 00:14:28.370
So we looked at proofs of how
people normally prove things and

00:14:28.371 --> 00:14:30.990
the simple proofs
are really based on this.

00:14:30.991 --> 00:14:33.150
This was enough to prove
most of the things.

00:14:34.446 --> 00:14:36.400
And so
the resulting formula is, so

00:14:36.401 --> 00:14:39.670
what we do is basically unfold
the recursive definitions.

00:14:39.671 --> 00:14:41.810
If I have a recursive
